@*
    The one field shape used everywhere (azure-ux-design-v1 §2):

        Label * ⓘ      [ value                    ]
                         helper text

    The label sits in its own 128px column — that column, not spacing, is what makes Azure's blade
    read denser at the same font size (§4). Callers therefore pass the input WITHOUT MudBlazor's
    floating `Label`, and give it an `aria-label` instead so assistive tech (and Playwright's
    getByLabel) can still resolve it.

    Info vs HelperText is a load-bearing split, not decoration: the ⓘ says WHY the field exists and
    what it affects, HelperText says HOW to fill it. Keeping them apart means neither crowds the
    other, and the format guidance stays visible rather than hiding behind a hover that does not
    exist on touch. Prefer examples over definitions.
*@

<div class="rm-form-row">
    <div class="rm-form-label">
        <span>@Label</span>
        @if (Required)
        {
            <span class="rm-form-label-req" aria-hidden="true">*</span>
        }
        @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Info))
        {
            <MudTooltip Text="@Info" Placement="Placement.Top">
                <MudIcon Icon="@Icons.Material.Outlined.Info" Class="rm-form-info"
                         aria-label="@($"About {Label}")" />
            </MudTooltip>
        }
    </div>
    <div class="rm-form-field">
        @ChildContent
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>Field label, rendered in the left column.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string Label { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>Adds the red required marker. Purely visual — validation lives on the input.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Required { get; set; }

    /// <summary>ⓘ tooltip: why this field exists / what it affects. Omit to render no icon.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Info { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The input itself, plus any inline action that belongs with it.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }
}
